    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Islam began with the revelations that Muslims believe the prophet Muhammad received from God through the angel Gabriel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> from 610, gathered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>: there is no god but God, and Muhammad is his messenger.</li><li>Within a century of Muhammad’s death in 632 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>; traders, scholars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> later carried Islam across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</li><li>Divided since the first century into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia traditions, it shaped law, learning, art and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabic-language">Arabic language</a> across a vast civilisation and is today the faith of about two billion people.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a> counts years from Muhammad’s migration from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 622; because it is a lunar calendar, its months move through the seasons, and the fast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> comes about eleven days earlier each year.</p></aside>
    </section>

