    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mecca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mecca.jpg" alt="Mecca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_of_Mecca1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saudipics.com, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Islam began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, a trading town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> that was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>, a sanctuary to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> came on pilgrimage to honour many gods. Muhammad, born there around 570 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">clan of Hashim</a> of the tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, was orphaned young and worked as a merchant. According to Islamic tradition, around 610, while meditating in a cave on Mount Hira, he received the first of a series of revelations from God through the angel Gabriel that continued until his death. These revelations, recited by him and memorised and written down by his followers, form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>.</p><p>Muhammad called the people of Mecca to worship the one God, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, the God of Abraham, to abandon idols, to care for the poor and to prepare for the Day of Judgement. His message won followers, among them his wife Khadija, his cousin Ali and his friend Abu Bakr, but met hostility from the leaders of Mecca. In 622 he and his followers migrated to the oasis of Yathrib, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, “the city of the prophet”. This migration, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a>, marks the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>.</p><p>In Medina Muhammad became the leader of a community, the umma, that was both religious and political. After years of conflict with Mecca, including the battles of Badr in 624 and Uhud in 625, he entered Mecca in 630 almost without fighting, cleared the Kaaba of idols and rededicated it to the one God. By his death in 632 much of Arabia had accepted Islam.</p><p>The question of who should lead the community after Muhammad shaped Islamic history. The majority accepted Abu Bakr as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, “successor”, followed by Umar, Uthman and Ali, the four “rightly guided” caliphs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> tradition. Others held that leadership belonged to Ali, Muhammad’s cousin and son-in-law, and his descendants: they became the Shia, “the party” of Ali. The killing of Ali’s son Husayn and his companions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> in 680 by the forces of the Umayyad caliph Yazid became the founding tragedy of Shia Islam, mourned every year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a>.</p><p>Under the first caliphs the Arab armies conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> within a generation, creating the framework within which Islam would grow into a world religion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/quran"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quran.jpg" alt="Quran" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Birmingham_Quran_manuscript_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Islam means “submission” to the will of God, and a Muslim is “one who submits”. Its central belief is tawhid, the absolute oneness of God, creator and sustainer of the universe, merciful and just, who has no partner and no equal. The declaration of faith, the shahada, states: “There is no god but God, and Muhammad is the messenger of God.”</p><p>Muslims believe that God sent prophets to every people, among them Adam, Noah, Abraham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, and that Muhammad is the last of them, the “seal of the prophets”. They believe in angels, in the scriptures revealed before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, among them the Torah and the Gospel, in God’s decree and in the Day of Judgement, when the dead will be raised and judged and enter paradise or hell.</p><p>The practice of Islam rests on five pillars: the shahada; the five daily prayers, salat, performed facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a>, the giving of a share of one’s wealth to the poor; fasting from dawn to sunset during the month of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>, the pilgrimage to Mecca, which every Muslim who is able should make once in a lifetime. Friday noon prayer is said together in the mosque. The two great festivals are Eid al-Fitr, at the end of Ramadan, and Eid al-Adha, during the hajj.</p><p>Islamic law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>, “the way”, covers worship, family life, commerce, crime and ethics. Jurists derived it from the Quran, the example of the prophet, consensus and reasoning, and by the 10th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> world recognised four schools of law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a>, Shafi’i and Hanbali, while the Shia followed their own Ja’fari school. The law forbids pork and alcohol, regulates marriage and inheritance and prohibits interest on loans.</p><p>Islam has developed many forms of thought and devotion. The theologians debated God’s attributes and human free will; the philosophers, among them al-Farabi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Ibn Rushd</a>, drew on Greek thought; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> sought direct, loving experience of God through prayer, music and meditation, organised from the 12th century in brotherhoods across the Muslim world. Shia Islam honours the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> descended from Ali, whom Twelver Shia believe will return as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rumi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rumi.jpg" alt="Rumi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D9%88%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%A7_%D8%A7%D8%AB%D8%B1_%D8%AD%D8%B3%DB%8C%D9%86_%D8%A8%D9%87%D8%B2%D8%A7%D8%AF_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hossein Behzad, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, “the recitation”, is for Muslims the literal word of God, revealed in Arabic to Muhammad over some twenty-three years. It consists of 114 chapters, suras, divided into verses, arranged broadly from longest to shortest rather than in the order of revelation. It speaks of God and creation, the prophets, the Day of Judgement, the laws of the community and the moral life, in a rhymed and rhythmic prose that Muslims regard as inimitable.</p><p>According to Islamic tradition, the Quran was first gathered into a single text under Abu Bakr and then, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Uthman around 650, a standard version was established and copies sent to the main cities of the empire, so that the text has remained essentially unchanged since. Fragments of the Quran on parchment from the 7th century, such as the Birmingham <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, confirm that its text was fixed early. Muslims learn the Quran in Arabic, memorise it, recite it in prayer and use translations, which they regard as interpretations rather than the Quran itself.</p><p>The second source of Islamic teaching is the sunna, the example of the prophet, known through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadith</a>, reports of his words and deeds transmitted by chains of narrators. In the 9th century scholars collected and evaluated them; the collections of al-Bukhari and Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj are the most respected among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunnis</a>, and the Shia have their own collections, which include the sayings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>.</p><p>Islamic learning produced a vast literature: commentaries on the Quran, such as those of al-Tabari in the 10th century; works of law and jurisprudence; the biography of the prophet, the sira, first written by Ibn Ishaq in the 8th century; works of theology and philosophy; and the poetry and teaching of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a>, from the treatises of al-Ghazali, whose Revival of the Religious Sciences reconciled law and mysticism around 1100, to the Persian poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Jalal al-Din Rumi</a> in the 13th century.</p><p>The study of the Quran and its language gave rise to Arabic grammar, lexicography and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, which became the most honoured art of the Islamic world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque.jpg" alt="Umayyad Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Umayyad Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyad_Mosque,_Damascus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Islam spread first with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a>. Within a century of Muhammad’s death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, conquered from 711, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. Conversion among the conquered peoples was gradual: Christians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> were permitted to keep their religions as protected peoples, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmis</a>, paying a special tax, and it took several centuries before Muslims formed the majority in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><p>The Umayyad caliphs ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> until 750; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, who replaced them, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, where the arts and sciences flourished. Political unity broke up after the 9th century into rival dynasties, among them the Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> of Egypt, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, but Islam continued to spread.</p><p>Beyond the reach of the caliphs, Islam travelled with merchants, scholars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a>. Across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> it reached the empires of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>: Muslim merchants lived in a town of their own beside the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> accepted the faith; along the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> it became the religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> towns; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> it spread to the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and then, from the 13th century, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and the Malay peninsula, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> today the largest Muslim country in the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> adopted Islam from the 10th century and carried it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and India.</p><p>Between the 15th and 17th centuries three great Muslim empires ruled much of the Islamic world: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453 and held the caliphate; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> of Iran, which made Twelver Shi’ism the religion of the state in 1501; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> of India. From the 18th century European powers came to rule most Muslim lands, and in the 20th century Muslim nations won independence. In 1924 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> abolished the caliphate.</p><p>Today about two billion people are Muslims, about a quarter of humanity; most live in Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and migration has created large communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. About 85 to 90 per cent are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and about 10 to 15 per cent Shia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/najaf"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/najaf.jpg" alt="Najaf" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shrine_of_Imam_Ali_Najaf_August_2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Goudarz.memar, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The mosque, masjid, “place of prostration”, is the centre of Muslim communal life, used for prayer, teaching and gathering. Its leaders include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> who leads the prayers and the preacher of the Friday sermon; Islam has no ordained priesthood, though scholars of religion, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">ulama</a>, have long held great authority as teachers, judges and interpreters of the law.</p><p>The umma, the worldwide community of believers, has been a powerful ideal of unity across peoples and languages, symbolised above all by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a>, during which pilgrims from every nation, dressed alike in simple white garments, perform the same rites at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>.</p><p>Islamic societies built institutions that served religious and public life: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a>, the college of religious learning, which spread from the 11th century; the waqf, the charitable endowment that funded mosques, schools, hospitals, fountains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>; courts led by judges, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadi">qadis</a>; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> lodges that offered hospitality and teaching.</p><p>Relations between religion and political power have taken many forms, from the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, in which the ruler was successor to the prophet, to sultanates that ruled with the support of the ulama, and modern states that range from secular republics to Islamic republics. Islamic law long governed family matters in most Muslim societies and continues to do so in many.</p><p>The place of women has varied greatly by period, region and school. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> gave women rights of inheritance and property, and women such as Khadija and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a> were central figures of early Islam; customs such as veiling and seclusion took many forms, and in modern times women’s rights and roles have been among the most debated questions in Muslim societies.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> and Shia Muslims share the core of their faith but differ in their understanding of leadership, in some laws and rituals, and in their sacred places: for Shia Muslims, the shrines of the imams at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najaf">Najaf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> are centres of pilgrimage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alhambra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alhambra.jpg" alt="Alhambra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dawn_Charles_V_Palace_Alhambra_Granada_Andalusia_Spain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jebulon, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Islam is the second-largest religion in the world and one of the great formative forces of world history. It created a civilisation that stretched from the Atlantic to the Pacific, held together by a shared faith, law and language of learning, in which goods, people and ideas travelled across three continents.</p><p>In the centuries of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and after, scholars working in Arabic preserved and translated Greek philosophy and science and made advances of their own in mathematics, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi">al-Khwarizmi</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>; in astronomy, optics and medicine, with Ibn al-Haytham and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a>; and in history and social thought, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>. Much of this learning passed into medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><p>Islamic architecture produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, completed in 691–692, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, the mosques of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">Calligraphy</a>, geometric ornament, carpets, ceramics and book painting flourished, and the literatures of Arabic, Persian, Turkish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> produced some of the world’s great poetry.</p><p>Arabic, the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, became the language of a vast region and gave words to Persian, Turkish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a>, Urdu, Malay and, through them and through Spain, to English: algebra, alcohol, admiral, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and sugar among them.</p><p>In modern times Islam has been at the centre of movements of revival, reform and political struggle, and of debates about its relation to the modern state. For two billion people today it remains, above all, a way of life defined by prayer, charity, fasting and the remembrance of God.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Islam, proclaimed by the prophet Muhammad in 7th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, teaches submission to the one God revealed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, expressed in the five pillars of faith, prayer, charity, fasting and pilgrimage. Spread by conquest, trade and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> teachers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, it shaped a great civilisation of law, learning and art and is today the faith of about two billion people.</em></p>

